<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">React Compiler S=
etup, Configuration &amp; Enterprise Deployment</h2>
<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 1: Warm-up =
&amp; Introduction</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm David, a Principal Frontend Engineer here at NAB. I've been=
 leading our frontend infrastructure and tooling efforts for the past 6 yea=
rs. Today I'd like to focus on the practical aspects of React Compiler adop=
tion - specifically around installation, configuration, and enterprise depl=
oyment strategies.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I noticed from your background t=
hat you have experience with large-scale React deployments. Can you tell me=
 about a time when you've had to roll out a new build tool or compiler acro=
ss multiple teams? What were the main challenges you encountered?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning David! Great to meet you. I actually led a similar initiative at m=
y previous fintech company where we rolled out TypeScript compilation acros=
s 15+ React applications spanning 8 different teams. The experience taught =
me a lot about the operational complexities of enterprise tooling changes.<=
/p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The biggest challenge wasn't tec=
hnical - it was <strong>organizational coordination</strong>. We had teams =
using different build tools: some on Create React App, others on custom Web=
pack configs, a few on Vite, and legacy apps still on Babel alone. Each tea=
m had different deployment pipelines, testing strategies, and even differen=
t Node.js versions.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The key lessons I learne=
d were:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Standardization before =
innovation</strong> - We had to establish baseline tooling consistency befo=
re introducing new capabilities</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Gradual migration strat=
egies</strong> - Big-bang approaches fail in enterprise environments</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Comprehensive testing a=
t each layer</strong> - Build-time, runtime, and integration testing</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Clear rollback procedur=
es</strong> - When you're dealing with production financial services, you n=
eed bulletproof fallback plans</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What made it particularly comple=
x was ensuring compatibility with our existing CI/CD infrastructure, mainta=
ining build performance SLAs, and coordinating releases across teams with d=
ifferent sprint cadences.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">For React Compiler specifically,=
 I've been following its development closely and have been experimenting wi=
th it in side projects. The installation and configuration aspects are dece=
ptively simple on the surface, but at enterprise scale with NAB's requireme=
nts, there are significant considerations around build pipeline integration=
, performance monitoring, and team coordination.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's exactly the kind of experience we're looking for. Enterprise rollouts =
are indeed more about coordination than pure technical implementation. Let'=
s dive into the technical specifics. If you were tasked with implementing R=
eact Compiler across NAB's frontend ecosystem, how would you approach the i=
nitial setup? We have applications using Next.js, Vite, custom Webpack conf=
igurations, and some legacy apps still on CRA. What would be your installat=
ion strategy?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: <str=
ong>Excellent question!</strong> This is where the rubber meets the road fo=
r enterprise adoption. Let me break down a comprehensive installation strat=
egy that addresses NAB's diverse build tool ecosystem.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>1. Environment Assessmen=
t &amp; Standardization Framework:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">First, I'd create a <strong>buil=
d tool audit and standardization framework</strong>:</p>
