<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Key Strategic Considerat=
ions for NAB:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Build Performance Impac=
t</strong>: Compiler adds 10-15% to build time initially - need to optimize=
 CI/CD pipelines</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Bundle Size Changes</st=
rong>: Initial increase due to runtime helpers, but long-term reduction fro=
m removing manual memoization</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Team Training Requireme=
nts</strong>: Different complexity levels based on existing build tool fami=
liarity</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Rollback Procedures</st=
rong>: Must maintain ability to disable compiler quickly if issues arise</l=
i>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Monitoring Integration<=
/strong>: Essential to track compiler effectiveness and catch regressions</=
li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The <strong>phased approach</str=
ong> ensures we can validate each step before proceeding, minimize risk to =
production systems, and provide adequate support for teams at different tec=
hnical maturity levels.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's a very thorough approach! I particularly like your tiered strategy and=
 the emphasis on automation. Now, let's dive deeper into one of the more co=
mplex scenarios. You mentioned Create React App in Tier 3. CRA is notorious=
ly difficult to configure without ejecting. We have several legacy applicat=
ions still using CRA that are business-critical and can't be easily migrate=
d. How would you handle React Compiler installation for these applications =
without ejecting and disrupting their current deployment pipelines?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: <str=
ong>Fantastic question!</strong> CRA presents unique challenges because of =
its "zero configuration" philosophy, but there are actually several sophist=
icated approaches to add React Compiler without ejecting. This is particula=
rly critical for business-critical applications where we need to minimize d=
eployment risk.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Strategy 1: CRACO (Creat=
e React App Configuration Override) - Recommended Approach</strong></p>
